 plus classes in cooking and  weaning eliza was ahead for her age  a year on things are different when  lockdown happened  it was gone overnight and  i know that kids are easy to adjust to  things but that must have had some kind  of effect  the fact that all of a sudden your whole  life has completely changed  the center she relied on so much has  become a medical hub  she's only been a handful of times all  year she's sure  it's had an impact she just wanted to  sit down and watch tv  all day she didn't want to play with her  toys she didn't want to do any arts and  crafts or anything she just wanted to  sit down and watch tv  she definitely stopped just kind of  going out there she just wanted to sit  it almost feels like you're not doing  enough  as a parent because  they're not doing what they're supposed  to be doing then or what you want them  to be able to do  it was really hard really hard  nurseries remained open for all children  during the second and third lockdowns  but during the first only the vulnerable  and children of key workers could attend  the harm wrought now coming to light  the early years alliance represents  those working with preschool children  in an exclusive survey of their members  three-quarters  said they'd seen changes in the learning  and development of children who stayed  at home during the first lockdown and  nearly 60  said fewer children are now at the  expected level of learning  at teddy bear corner in warwickshire not  all children returned  for those that did the impact was stark  some of them came back  and had thrived in lockdown but then  other children  it kind of gone the other way their  speech and language had kind of gone  back to where it was before they started  with us  and the children move up to us they  mainly all potty trained  we noticed that they kind of regressed  during lockdown and came back and  were kind of back needed reminding to go  to the toilet  the children who haven't necessarily had  that support from us this  year because of covid they're not  necessarily ready  emotionally and socially to go into  school i don't know if they'll ever get  that time back  and are you worried about some of these  children going forward yes the ones  that are going to school in september if  they're the ones that have got  you know difficulties  we don't have long now to put those  things the provisions in place that we  normally would  but they're most worried about the  already disadvantaged  some have fallen disproportionately  behind  in fact nearly half of early years  practitioners said the gap between the  worst off children and their peers  has widened since the start of the  pandemic that is  brilliant what's up guys sue's run this  nursery for 25 years  there's a mix of children here from a  wide range of homes  leveling the playing field has always  been paramount but it's never been  harder  i think lockdown did have a profound  effect on them  the equalities have always been there  but i think it's going to  to get worse to get worse to the  detriment of all the children we look  after  this generation could well be a  partially lost generation from some  things  and in all your 25 years of working in  the sector have you ever  seen something that would have such an  impact on children this one  no the difference in outcomes is likely  to be down to a complex mix of factors  cramped living conditions perhaps or  parents working multiple jobs that can't  be done from home  experts in this field are keen to  emphasize that all families are  different and that the pandemic has  taken a deep toll  on all sorts of different people for all  sorts of different reasons  but many fear the really detrimental  impacts will last longest  in the most vulnerable that children  will carry them through their time at  school  entrenching already deep social and  educational inequalities  you made it elsie may started attending  this nursery during the first lockdown  when her family qualified for extra  support  it was probably  the lowest point i've ever been as a  parent nothing was as hard as  as the the first lock down  philomena's a single parent and her  elder son has special needs  look right forward she says the extra  nursery time for elsie may literally  kept the family together  but that time has still taken its toll  well she's definitely regressed  um milestones that she was hit in  um toilet in things like that of you  know  we've she's massively regressed in them  to the point where you know i've had to  have the healthiest involved now you  worry as well that the impact of this  will stay with her with her forever yeah  i truly believe it will she used to be  such a sparky little thing  i do think for elsie may it's  changed her personality it's changed her  little way of thinking  but others are more isolated this is  talitha and her twins thomas  and teguin hello morning  how are you i'm fine thank you when we  met them last year life was just  settling down  they'd been homeless for a few months  early in the twins lives  a baby bank delivery another huge help  it's a bit overwhelming in there because  i didn't expect all this stuff  when you're like a single mum you don't  want anyone to think you can't cook you  don't want people to think you can  do the things that you need to do  there's a bit of you that feels like you  can't do it  but you can do it because it takes a lot  to ask for help  it takes more to ask for help than it  does to sit and struggle on your own  money was tight but talitha was positive  take her in the red  but a year later she didn't feel up to  catching up with us on camera  like so many she's found the pandemic  very difficult and very isolating  she was happy for us to report that the  twins are well but the family  have barely been out for a year as clear  as sign as any of covid's cruel  but quiet impacts  my  [Music]  there are many hidden tolls to this  pandemic  the worst may not be 